# Application key rotation and re-encryption

## Why

The application key encrypts four database columns and keys three HMACs. A
copy of the key that has left its custody (old environment backups, an old
application directory, a workstation) can decrypt those columns and forge those
HMACs, so the key has to be rotatable — and rotating it must not make a single
encrypted value unreadable.

Laravel decrypts with the current key and then each previous key, so setting a
new key with the old one as a previous key keeps every row readable. That only
holds while the old key stays configured: a rotation that stops there has not
retired the old key at all. The rows have to be rewritten under the new key
before the old one can be removed.

## Rules

1. **Every encrypted model cast is registered.** A model cast to `encrypted` or
   `encrypted:<type>` must be listed in `config/app_key_rotation.php`.
   `AppKeyReencryptionTest` scans `app/**/Models/` and fails until it is, so a new
   encrypted column cannot silently keep a retired key alive.
2. **A value is in exactly one state.** `current` decrypts with the current key
   alone; `previous` decrypts only with a previous key; `undecryptable` decrypts
   with no configured key. `security:app-key-reencrypt` reports all three.
3. **Undecryptable stops everything.** While any value is undecryptable the
   command writes nothing and exits 1. Never "skip" such a row — it is evidence
   of a key that was lost or a value that was corrupted, and rewriting the others
   would hide it.
4. **One row per transaction.** The rewrite re-reads the value under a row lock,
   encrypts the identical plaintext with the current key, proves the result
   decrypts with the current key alone, and writes through the query builder so
   `updated_at` and model observers do not move. Never batch rows into one
   transaction and never write through Eloquent.
5. **A previous key is retired only when 0 values depend on it.** The condition is
   `previous_key_values = 0` and `undecryptable = 0` on the running configuration
   (`previous_keys_removable: true`). Never remove the previous key on the
   strength of a dry run taken before the rewrite.
6. **Nothing secret leaves the process.** The command prints counts only — never
   a key, a plaintext or a ciphertext — and the audit row
   `APP_KEY_VALUES_REENCRYPTED` carries counts only. No key value goes into the
   repository, a report, a sprint doc, Obsidian, a CI artifact or a terminal.
   Compare keys by salted hash, never by value.
7. **The HMAC users fail closed and are not covered by previous keys.**
   `DoctorAppLoginService` (login ticket, 60 s; challenge, 120 s),
   `DoctorDeviceEnrollmentService` (pairing code, 15 min) and `RmePaymentIntent`
   (payment form intent) key their HMAC on the current key only. After rotation
   an in-flight login retries, an unexpired pairing code must be re-issued, and an
   open payment form is refused and must be reopened — a resubmission can never
   be accepted as a second payment. Rotate in a window where none is in flight.
   These files are registered in `config/app_key_rotation.php`
   (`current_key_only_consumers`) with their rotation behaviour; a test fails when
   another file under `app/` reads the application key directly, and the ticket
   and pairing-code refusals are exercised with a real key rotation.
8. **Framework state that follows previous keys.** Encrypted cookies and signed
   URLs are verified against the current and every previous key, and the response
   re-encrypts cookies with the current key, so active sessions survive the
   rotation and are migrated by the next request. Once the previous key is
   retired, a cookie or signed URL issued before rotation and not refreshed since
   is no longer valid. Check `session.encrypt` before rotating: if it is true,
   stored session payloads follow the same rule.
9. **Old backups keep the old key's ciphertext.** A database dump taken before
   rotation decrypts only with the old key. Keep the old key in sealed custody for
   at least the backup retention window; restoring such a dump means configuring
   the old key as a previous key and running the command with `--apply`, never
   editing rows by hand. `AppKeyReencryptionRecoveryTest` pins this: with the old
   key retired a restored pre-rotation backup is refused and nothing is written;
   with the escrowed key it is recovered and re-encrypted.
10. **Rotation is a supervised operation.** Generate the key on the host, write it
    into the environment file in place (same inode, owner and mode), rebuild the
    configuration cache as the runtime user, restart the queue worker and reload
    PHP-FPM, then run the command with `--apply` and verify. Rollback before the
    previous key is retired restores the old key, keeps the new one as a previous
    key and moves the rewritten values back. After retirement there is no rollback
    without the retired key, which is why retirement is a separate, later step.
11. **Never delete the old key before the data and its dependants are safe.**
    That means: 0 values depend on it, the backup retention window for pre-rotation
    dumps is covered by sealed custody, and no other copy of the environment file
    still carries it as the live key.

## Custody, inventory and the production release gate (G7-B gate closure, 2026-10-30)

12. **Two independently accountable custodians before any production rotation.** One
    IT custodian and one management custodian, each holding one independently
    encrypted escrow copy under their own passphrase. A custodian is appointed only
    by an explicit owner decision recorded in the G7-B report; a proposal is not an
    appointment. **An Android APK signing-key custody assignment (rules 145/146) is
    never evidence of application-key custody** — different key, different scope, and
    its "Custodian 2/3" are storage media, not people.
13. **Recovery is two-person.** The holder of a passphrase types it at the GPG prompt;
    the root operator runs the tool. When the IT custodian is also the operator, the
    management custodian witnesses and signs the custody log. Passphrases never pass
    through chat, argv or files.
14. **The environment-copy inventory is per copy and covers archives.** Every copy
    carrying a key line — plain files, empty templates, and members inside tar/zip
    archives — gets an opaque id. The id→path mapping lives only in a root-only
    manifest on the host (dir 0700, file 0600); a public document carries ids, classes,
    modes, months and key states only, never a path or a filename. "Is it needed?" is
    answered by checking every on-host database dump's ciphertext MACs in memory
    against each distinct key, never by assumption.
15. **The production rotation gate is a conjunction.** Rotation may execute only when
    SECOND_CUSTODIAN=VERIFIED, ENV_COPY_INVENTORY=VERIFIED, RETENTION_DECISION=APPROVED,
    OLD_KEY_RECOVERY=VERIFIED, NEW_KEY_RECOVERY=VERIFIED, REENCRYPTION_TEST=PASS,
    ROLLBACK_TEST=PASS, BACKUP_RESTORE=PASS and PRODUCTION_EXECUTION_APPROVAL=EXPLICIT.
    One unmet condition is NO-GO. A rehearsal with machine-generated passphrases proves
    the tool, not the custody: OLD_KEY_RECOVERY is verified only from the real
    custodians' copies.
16. **Rehearse on a disposable copy, never on production.** The A0→R2 rehearsal restores
    the newest pre-deploy backup into a scratch database, passes keys through the process
    environment only, and drops the scratch database on every exit path. Production
    configuration, database and the active environment file are never written.
